---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";

const actions = [
  ["Vitoria", "21 June 1813", "second in command"],
  ["The Pyrenees", "July–August 1813", "second in command"],
  ["Nivelle", "10 November 1813", "second in command"],
  ["Nive", "9–13 December 1813", "second in command"],
  ["Garris", "15 February 1814", "the 39th named in Wellington's despatch"],
  ["Orthes", "27 February 1814", "commanded the regiment"],
  ["Aire", "2 March 1814", "engaged"],
  ["Toulouse", "10 April 1814", "third in command"],
];
---
<Base
  title="Australia"
  eyebrow="18 September 1826 — the arrival"
  dek="A major of the 39th Foot who had commanded his regiment at Orthes came out in charge of the guard on a convict ship, with his wife and baby daughter in the cabin. He never went home.">

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Found 13 September 2026</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Eight days before the ship</h2>
    <p class="lede">Chatham St Mary the Virgin baptised <strong>Charlotte D'Arcy</strong> on
    <strong>31 March 1826</strong> — born 29 April 1825, father entered as
    <strong>“George Pitt D'Arcy, Bt: Major 39th Foot”</strong>, mother <strong>Maria</strong>, abode
    Chatham.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:1rem">On <strong>8 April 1826</strong>, eight days later, the surgeon of the
    convict ship <em>England</em> received aboard a detachment of the 39th commanded by
    "<em>George Pitt D'Arcy, B. Major</em>". They christened the baby, and then they sailed.
    <a href={u("/chatham")}>The Chatham register →</a> <span class="chip doc">documented</span></p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <p class="lede">This is the part of the pedigree that does not need defending, and it turns out
    to be the best part. The man at the bottom of the English line is not a footnote: he is a
    Peninsular War officer who commanded the 39th Regiment in a general action, and the colony's
    newspapers said so when he died.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">The ship</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">The convict ship <em>England</em>, 1826</h2>

    <p>The surgeon superintendent's journal and the ship's record give the voyage almost day by day.
    <strong>The journal has now been identified at the National Archives</strong> and it is
    <span class="mono">ADM 101/26/1</span> — <em>"Medical journal of the England, convict ship, for
    18 March to 29 September 1826 by <strong>George Thomson, Surgeon and Superintendent</strong>"</em>.
    Until 10 September 2026 this archive cited it only as "the surgeon's journal". It names Major
    D'Arcy four times.</p>

    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>8 April 1826</td><td>"Received on board a detachment of the 39th regt. of foot
            <strong>commanded by George Pitt D'Arcy, B. Major</strong>, consisting of
            <strong>39 men</strong> (including 2 sergeants and 3 corporals), <strong>6 women and
            7 children</strong>. Victualled on board and found fit for embarkation."
            <div class="small" style="margin-top:.3rem"><strong>Two corrections here.</strong> This
            page previously said <em>30 men</em>, from a secondary account; the catalogue's
            transcription of the journal itself says <strong>39</strong>, and the coincidence with
            the regiment's number is presumably how the error started. And his rank on the day is
            <strong>B. Major</strong> — brevet major, not yet substantive.</div></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Cabin</td><td>"Cabin passengers included <strong>Mrs. D'Arcy and family</strong> and
            Mrs. Reay." — <em>the wife and children sailed with him</em></td></tr>
          <tr><td>14–21 April</td><td>148 male convicts taken aboard at Woolwich from the
            <em>Justitia</em> and <em>Ganymede</em> hulks</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Spring 1826</td><td>the phrenologist Mr De Ville came aboard and examined all 148
            convicts, "and gave a memorandum of the inferred character of each individual"</td></tr>
          <tr><td>3 May</td><td>down the river, anchored at Gravesend</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>6 May</strong></td><td>pilot discharged <strong>in the Downs</strong></td></tr>
          <tr><td>17 May</td><td>the convicts <strong>broke into his luggage</strong> — "a crate of
            dinner ware, and a box of soup stored in the fore hold, <strong>belonging to major
            D'Arcy</strong>, and stolen several articles". John Wells and William Kerr were suspected
            and tried by petty officers</td></tr>
          <tr><td>mid-voyage</td><td><strong>a mutiny scare.</strong> Three knives were traced to a
            convict who had threatened the surgeon. "<strong>Major D'Arcy gave the necessary orders to
            the guard how to act in case of an alarm</strong>", and the master armed the crew with
            cutlasses. The surgeon then told the prison deck that if violence were offered he "would
            instantly direct them to be put to death"</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>16 August</strong></td><td>"guard, crew and convicts healthy … <strong>Major
            D'Arcy recovered from his attack of the gout</strong>"</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>18 September</strong></td><td>arrived <strong>Port Jackson</strong> — 135
            days, <strong>no deaths</strong></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.3rem">
      <strong>The gout closes a loop twenty-three years wide.</strong> On 16 August 1826 the surgeon
      of the <em>England</em> noted that Major D'Arcy had recovered from an attack of gout, somewhere
      in the Southern Ocean.
      <br /><br />
      In July 1849, at Parramatta, a coroner's jury sat on his body and returned a verdict putting the
      cause of death at "<em>gout which had flown to the head and caused apoplexy</em>".
      <br /><br />
      Two records, twenty-three years and half a world apart, neither aware of the other, describing
      the same man's same complaint — and the second one killed him.
      <a href={u("/papers")}>The obituary →</a>
    </div>

    <p class="small" style="margin-top:.8rem">Master: John Reay. Surgeon superintendent:
    George Thomson, R.N., whose first convict voyage this was and who called it one of the more
    troubled of 1826. Ship built at Chepstow, 1813.</p>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.3rem">
      <strong>The children's birthplaces now read straight through.</strong>
      <strong>Charlotte Maria, Chatham, 1825</strong> — she is the baby in the cabin.
      <strong>Catherine Maria, Parramatta, 1827.</strong>
      <strong>George Lindsay, Parramatta, 24 August 1829</strong>, christened at St James's, Sydney,
      on 5 November. Two years and half a world between the first and the second.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Before Australia</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Thirty-four years in the 39th, and a gold medal that went to the wrong man</h2>

    <p>He served in the West Indies, in North and South America, in the Mediterranean, and in the
    Peninsula under Wellington from 1811 to the end of the war. His obituary lists the actions:</p>

    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:1.1rem">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Action</th><th class="m">Date</th><th>His part</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {actions.map(([a, d, r]) => (
            <tr><td>{a}</td><td class="m">{d}</td><td>{r}</td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p style="margin-top:1.2rem">At <strong>Orthes</strong> on 27 February 1814 the command of the
    39th fell to him because <strong>Lieutenant-Colonel Bruce was wounded early in the action</strong>.
    He received a gold medal for it. And then this:</p>

    <blockquote>
      The Major received a gold medal for his prominent services in the battle of Orthes, but
      <strong>singular to relate, after a lapse of four years, the reward of his merit having been
      sent by mistake to Lieutenant-Colonel Bruce.</strong>
    </blockquote>

    <p>Four years to reach the man who earned it, because it had been posted to the officer whose
    wound gave him the command in the first place.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">22 July 1849</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">The obituary, and what it does not quite say</h2>

    <blockquote>
      At Parramatta, on Sunday morning, the 22nd of July, 1849, aged 69, <strong>Major George Pitt
      D'Arcy, formerly of the 39th Regiment of Foot; a brave and gallant soldier.</strong> Major
      D'Arcy served in the West Indies, the Mediterranean, through the whole of the Peninsular War,
      North America, and in this colony. He received a medal for commanding his regiment at the
      battle of Orthes.
      <footer class="small" style="font-style:normal;margin-top:.6rem">— <em>Bell's Life in Sydney and
      Sporting Reviewer</em>, Saturday 28 July 1849, p. 2 ·
      <span class="mono">nla.news-article59769271</span>. The same notice ran in the
      <em>Sydney Morning Herald</em> (24 and 26 July), the <em>Maitland Mercury</em> (28 July) and the
      <em>Colonial Times</em> at Hobart (17 August).</footer>
    </blockquote>

    <p>Then the writer goes on, and the tone changes:</p>

    <blockquote>
      The decline of a life spent in the service of his country was not, we deeply regret to say,
      passed in the paths of pleasantness; and though philosophy taught Major D'Arcy to sustain
      sorrow and trouble uncomplainingly … yet those who were intimately acquainted with him had
      reason to know that the canker of disappointment was at his heart's core, and his smile was too
      often only the shroud of buried hopes. He died at the house of Mr. Walford, one of his
      staunchest friends, whose kindness to the deceased gentleman increased in proportion to his need
      of it. … <strong>one who in his lifetime tried to be the friend of all, and never injured anyone
      but himself.</strong>
    </blockquote>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <strong>Read that carefully and label it honestly.</strong> "Never injured anyone but himself",
      dying in another man's house, with a friend's kindness increasing "in proportion to his need of
      it" — this is a Victorian obituarist writing round something, most likely drink, debt, or both.
      It is <span class="chip inf">inferred</span>, from tone and euphemism, and it is not a fact.
      What <em>is</em> stated is that he died in another man's house and that his affairs had gone
      badly. The archive will not turn a delicate paragraph into a diagnosis.
    </div>

    <p style="margin-top:1.2rem">Because the death was sudden there was an <strong>inquest</strong>.
    The jury put the cause at "<strong>gout which had flown to the head, and caused apoplexy</strong>"
    — in modern terms, most probably a stroke.</p>

    <p class="small" style="margin-top:.9rem">One discrepancy: every 1849 notice gives his age as
    <strong>69</strong>, which puts his birth at about 1780. The family tree says 18 February 1783.
    <a href={u("/open-questions")}>Queued →</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Corrected</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Two wives, and neither of them married him in 1810</h2>

    <p>The family tree records him marrying <em>Maria or Mary Le Meassier</em> and
    <em>Maria (Catherine) White</em> — both at Chatham, both in 1810. A man cannot marry two women in
    one town in one year, and the archive flagged it as unresolved. It is now resolved, and the tree
    is wrong twice over:</p>

    <div class="grid two" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>First: <strong>Miss Ludlam</strong>, September 1805</h3>
        <p>Not 1810, not 1806, and not <em>Le Mesurier</em> — <strong>Le Mesurier was her mother's
        maiden name</strong>, which is exactly how it got into the tree as though it were hers. She
        is the mother of <strong>Frederick Robert</strong>, christened at Chatham on 2 July 1811.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Second: <strong>Maria White</strong>, about 1819, County Wicklow</h3>
        <p>In <strong>Ireland</strong>, not Chatham, and nine years later than the tree says. She is
        the mother of Charlotte Maria, Catherine Maria and <strong>George Lindsay</strong> — and she
        is the "Mrs. D'Arcy" in the cabin of the <em>England</em>.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.4rem">
      <strong>And the first marriage is now dated from a contemporary printing.</strong> The
      <em>Salisbury and Winchester Journal</em> of <strong>23 September 1805</strong>, page 3, carried
      the notice — and it identifies the groom twice over:

      <p style="margin-top:.7rem"><strong>"Captain George d'Arcy"</strong>, son of
      <strong>"Lieut Colonel d'Arcy"</strong>, married <strong>"Miss Ludlam"</strong>.</p>

      <p style="margin-top:.7rem">That is worth more than the marriage date. It is an independent,
      contemporary, printed statement that <strong>George Pitt D'Arcy's father was a
      Lieutenant-Colonel D'Arcy</strong> — which is exactly what Robert D'Arcy was in 1805, on his way
      to Major-General of Engineers. Until now the father–son join rested on the family tree. It now
      rests on a newspaper printed while both men were alive.
      <a href={u("/hornby")}>What that does and does not prove →</a></p>
    </div>

    <p style="margin-top:1.4rem">All of which settles a question this archive had left open:
    <strong>Frederick Robert D'Arcy the surveyor is a half-brother</strong>, not a full brother, to
    the direct ancestor. Twenty-four years and two mothers separate them — and
    <strong>George Lindsay's own christening entry proves it</strong>: St James's, Sydney,
    5 November 1829, "father George Pitt Darcy, mother <strong>Maria</strong>".</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">A discrepancy worth the space</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">The headstone says the 9th</h2>
    <p>Frederick Robert D'Arcy's monumental inscription at <strong>Toowong</strong> reads:
    <em>"Eldest son of Major George Pitt D'ARCY late of <strong>9th</strong> Regiment"</em>. Every
    other source says the <strong>39th</strong>.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:.9rem">The 39th wins, and not narrowly. Six independent newspapers in 1849
    — in Sydney, Maitland and Hobart — say 39th; the surgeon's journal of the <em>England</em> in
    1826 says 39th; <strong>Frederick Robert's own death notice</strong>, placed by his family two
    days after he died, says "Major George Pitt D'Arcy, <strong>39th Regiment</strong>"; his
    half-brother's obituary in 1901 says "the old 39th regiment"; and his service is given as
    <strong>34 years in the 39th</strong>. The same transcript also dates his death to 10 September
    where the notice says the 19th, so it is wrong twice. A monumental mason cutting "39" and losing the 3,
    or a transcriber reading a worn stone, accounts for it easily.</p>
    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.1rem">
      Recorded rather than quietly corrected, because the next person to find that headstone will
      have the same moment of doubt, and should find the answer here instead of repeating the work.
      <a href={u("/method")}>Method →</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">His brothers</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Three corps, and a boy dead at nineteen</h2>
    <p>He was the third of the ten children of Major-General Robert D'Arcy of the Royal Engineers,
    and the family put its sons into three different corps.</p>

    <div class="grid two" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Joseph, and the Persian officers</h3>
        <p><strong>Lieutenant-Colonel Joseph D'Arcy</strong> (1780–1848), Royal Artillery, went to
        Persia in 1811 with Sir Gore Ouseley's mission to reform the Shah's army, and the Persian
        victory over the Russians at Solṭānābād in February 1812 was credited largely to his guns. He
        brought five Persian students back to England — and the <em>Records of the Royal Military
        Academy</em> record what happened next:</p>
        <blockquote style="margin:.8rem 0 0">Lieutenant-Colonel D'Arcy, Royal Artillery, who had some
        Persian Officers under his care … having applied to have them educated at the Royal Military
        Academy, the Master-General referred to the Lieutenant-Governor … who replied that great
        inconvenience would result were they instructed with the Cadets, but they might attend the
        Musters as private pupils; <strong>they were ultimately placed with Mr. Christie, 4th
        Mathematical Master.</strong></blockquote>
        <p class="small" style="margin-top:.6rem">His eldest son was named <strong>George Abbas Kooli
        D'Arcy</strong> at the Shah's request, and became governor of the Gambia and of the Falkland
        Islands.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Constantine, Royal Engineers</h3>
        <p>Christened at <strong>St Michael, Barbados, on 18 February 1786</strong> — the record names
        Robert and Jane. He followed his father into the Engineers: second lieutenant 1 February 1804,
        lieutenant 1 March 1805, and then Connolly's Roll simply says
        <strong>"Died at Chatham, 6 5 1805"</strong>. Five weeks after his promotion, aged about
        nineteen.</p>
        <p class="small" style="margin-top:.6rem">The family tree says he died in Barbados. He was
        <em>born</em> there; the Corps register says he died at Chatham.
        <a href={u("/open-questions")}>Queued →</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk" style="border-bottom:0">
    <div class="lab">His eldest son</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Frederick Robert D'Arcy — the surveyor who helped map Melbourne</h2>
    <p>Born Chatham 1811, christened there 2 July; <em>withdrawn from Sandhurst by his family to
    emigrate</em>; sailed for Sydney on the <strong>Albion</strong>, 3 November 1828, two years behind
    his father. About 1830 he became a draughtsman in the New South Wales Surveyor-General's
    Department under <strong>Major Thomas Mitchell</strong>.</p>

    <blockquote>
      In late 1836 Governor Bourke sent three surveyors — Robert Russell, <strong>Frederick
      D'Arcy</strong> and William Darke — with thirty-six convicts to the Port Phillip District, to
      chart the shore of Port Phillip Bay and plot the course of the Yarra River.
    </blockquote>

    <p>That survey is the ground plan of <strong>Melbourne</strong>. He worked afterwards in the Blue
    Mountains, at Rylestone and Merriwa, and finally in Queensland; his modern biographer describes an
    explorer of rougher country than any other early mainland surveyor, and a man who "lurched from
    one disaster to another". He went through the Insolvent Court at Hartley in 1852.
    <a href={u("/papers")}>The papers →</a></p>

    <p style="margin-top:.9rem">His death notice settles the date, which the Toowong transcript gets
    wrong along with the regiment:</p>
    <blockquote>D'ARCY.—On the <strong>19th September</strong>, at his residence, <strong>Spring
    Hill</strong>, Frederick Robert D'Arcy, <strong>licensed surveyor</strong>, eldest son of the late
    <strong>Major George Pitt D'Arcy, 39th Regiment</strong>, aged <strong>63</strong>.
    [Sydney papers please copy.]
    <footer class="small" style="font-style:normal;margin-top:.6rem">— <em>The Brisbane Courier</em>,
    Tuesday 21 September 1875, p. 2 · <span class="mono">nla.news-article1381942</span></footer></blockquote>
    <p>He was buried at <strong>Toowong</strong> — in the same city and the same decade as the
    half-brother whose line this archive follows. <a href={u("/brisbane")}>Brisbane →</a></p>

    <p class="small" style="margin-top:1rem">Andy Macqueen, <em>Frederick Robert D'Arcy: Colonial
    Surveyor, Explorer and Artist, c.1809–1875</em>. Macqueen dates his birth to about 1809; the
    Chatham christening of 2 July 1811 and the "aged 63" of his death notices both support 1811–12.
    Recorded, not resolved.</p>
  </section>
</Base>
